An Irrepressible Myth
For all the high-minded rhetoric about the common law and nature of judging in Swift and Erie, one should not forget that, at bottom, these cases were interpreting a federal statute. The Rules of Decision Act, after all, provides explicit statutory instructions to the federal courts on when they must use state law. Of course, to say that instructions are explicit is not to say that they are clear. The Rules of Decision Act is as ambiguous as it is brief. Recall the words of the statute as it existed at the time of Swift and, a century later, at the time of Erie:

The laws of the several States, except where the Constitution, treaties, or statutes of the United States otherwise require or provide, shall be regarded as rules of decision in trials at common law, in the courts of the United States, in cases where they apply.

The Rules of Decision Act (“RDA”) is a mere 45 words. In length, it is closer to a haiku poem than a typical federal statute. Yet Swift and Erie each sought to disambiguate only one phrase of the whole. They gave answers to the question: What does “The laws of the several States” mean? Swift said that “laws” did not include judicial decisions; Erie overruled Swift and said that “laws” includes judge-made law.

As later cases built upon Erie, they ran up against further ambiguities in the RDA, each more subtle than the last. Indeed, we can organize the cases in this chapter around four questions of statutory interpretation that flow from the original text of the RDA. Each question seeks to clarify the meaning of a single phrase:

First: What does “The laws of the several States” mean?

Second: What does “at common law” mean?

Third: What does “except where the Constitution, treaties, or statutes of the United States otherwise require or provide” mean?

Fourth: What does “rules of decision” mean?

So far, we have answered the first question. Under Erie, federal judges cannot independently craft common law rules to decide a claim for breach of contract, or personal injury, etc. If a federal judge is going to use common law
