to decide such a claim, she must follow the common law of a state. A federal judge in Tennessee, for example, will decide as if she were a state judge in Tennessee, following Tennessee law governing how to decide a breach of contract claim, or personal injury claim, etc.

But what if the claim is not for breach of contract, or personal injury, but a claim for rescission of a contract, or to abate a nuisance? These are not claims “at common law,” but claims in equity. This brings us to the second question. You may recall from the discussion of the history of pleading in Chapter 3, and from the discussion of the Seventh Amendment right to jury trial in Chapter 5, the long-standing distinction between common law and equity in the United States. When the Supreme Court decided Erie, the RDA said that state law will provide the rules of decision “in trials at common law.” It said nothing about cases in equity. What are federal courts supposed to do when deciding claims for equitable relief? Ruhlin v. New York Life Insurance, which we will read about shortly, would answer this question.

Next, consider the situation that arises when the choice is not merely between following state caselaw or federal caselaw, but between following state law and following federal codified law—statutes, rules, and so on. This presents us with the third question: What does “except where the Constitution, treaties, or statutes of the United States otherwise require or provide” mean? When the federal codified law is the law creating the cause of action under which the plaintiff is suing, then the RDA gives a simple answer: Federal law provides the rule of decision. If a plaintiff’s complaint alleges an antitrust conspiracy and seeks relief under Section 1 of the Sherman Act, 15 U.S.C. § 1, federal law is all the federal court needs. Of course, a plaintiff could choose to forgo federal codified law, even if it exists. If a plaintiff files a complaint in federal court on the basis of diversity jurisdiction, and the complaint alleges an antitrust conspiracy and seeks relief only under Section 3 of the Illinois Antitrust Act, 740 ILCS 10/3, the federal court will apply the Illinois Antitrust Act as the rule of decision.

What about the Federal Rules? The Rules are, in a literal sense, judge-made law, as they are promulgated by federal judges, but the federal rulemaking process is authorized by an act of Congress—the Rules Enabling Act. Does this mean that state law does not apply when a Federal Rule does? We see the Court give an answer to this question in Sibbach v. Wilson & Co., which appears later in this chapter. But this question does not go away, and we shall see
