the Court revisit it over and over—in Cohen v. Beneficial Industrial Loan Corp., Hanna v. Plumer, and most recently in Shady Grove v. Allstate.

Finally, consider the converse situation, when the choice is between following state codified law and following federal law. After reading Swift and Erie, one might think this is the easiest question of all. For all their differences, Swift and Erie agreed on one principle: if the plaintiff’s claim is based on a state statute—not the common law or equity—then a federal court must follow the state statute, not choose a different rule to follow. Yet the past eight decades of experience have confounded this seemingly easy principle.

Why? This brings us to the fourth question: What does “rules of decision” mean? As the words suggest, a rule of decision provides the basis upon which the court determines who wins. What duty of care did Erie Railroad owe to Tompkins? The RDA says that state law provides the answer. What are the elements that Tompkins must prove to prevail on his claim for personal injury? The RDA says that state law provides the answer. Does Tompkins lose if the railroad can show he was contributorily negligent? The RDA says that state law provides the answer. Erie tells us this much.

But state law includes more than causes of action for tort. States have their own rules of procedure, rules of evidence, statutes of limitation, rights to jury trial, and so on. None of these things enumerate the elements of a cause of action. But as we have seen throughout this book, procedure is often the basis upon which a court decides who wins and who loses. Yet no justice interpreting the RDA has ever claimed that it requires federal courts to follow state procedural law. As Justice Reed said in his concurrence in Erie: “The line between procedural and substantive law is hazy, but no one doubts federal power over procedure.”

